course: dictionary
lesson: 10908
title: Visual
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Visual is an adjective, and it means to do with seeing or sight. Your eyes give you your visual sense. «Visual aids such as slides, pictures or objects help the audience follow.» Here visual aids are things you look at to help you understand, not things you hear.
explain: Visual can also be a noun, a picture, chart or video that shows or explains something. In maths, a bar model is a visual, a drawing that lets you see the problem. You can say, «Use a visual to show your idea.»
explain: Look at the words around visual to check its meaning. In «Goalball is played 3 against 3 by athletes with visual impairments», the word impairments tells you something is harder, so a visual impairment is a problem with seeing. Everyone wears eyeshades so the game is fair.
fact: Visual means to do with seeing or sight.
fact: As a noun, a visual is a picture, chart or video used to show or explain something.
fact: «Visual aids» are things like slides, pictures or objects that help an audience follow.
fact: «Visual impairments» means problems with seeing.
fact: Do not read slides out word for word; use them to support what you say.
exercise: «Visual aids such as slides, pictures or objects help the audience follow.» What does visual mean here? | To do with running | To do with tasting | *To do with seeing | To do with hearing
exercise: Which of these is a visual? | *A bar model drawn to show a maths problem | A whispered secret | A song you can only hear | A smell from the kitchen
exercise: «Goalball is played 3 against 3 by athletes with visual impairments.» What does visual impairment mean? | Trouble with running | Trouble with sleeping | Trouble with hearing | *Trouble with seeing
test: Our visual sense uses which body part? | Nose | *Eyes | Tongue | Ears
test: Why is a visual useful in class? | It makes the lesson shorter | *It helps you see and understand an idea | It replaces the teacher | It stops you from listening
exam: In which phrase is visual a noun? | *a visual to help the class understand | visual effects in a film | visual impairments | visual sense
